# Line endings for bytes that are load-bearing.
#
# The repo had no .gitattributes, so with core.autocrlf=true (the default on
# Derek's Windows checkouts) git rewrote LF to CRLF on checkout for everything.
# For most sources that is harmless. For two classes of file it is not:
#
#   1. Generated artifacts that get HASHED and PUBLISHED. workbench.html is
#      sha256'd and compared against the live X-Workbench-Sha256 header by
#      scripts/workbench-verify-live.mjs, which reads the local file as a raw
#      Buffer. A CRLF working copy makes that check report a false mismatch
#      against a deployment that is byte-correct — measured 2026-07-30: raw
#      local 2bb23be9…, LF-normalized 976f51cc…, and 976f51cc… is exactly what
#      AM4 was serving. A false "stale" reading invites an unnecessary
#      republish and, worse, teaches the operator to distrust a passing gate.
#
#   2. Files that ship to Linux and are parsed there. A systemd unit with CRLF
#      passes a literal carriage return into ExecStart=; a shell script with
#      CRLF fails on the shebang. These currently survive only because
#      autocrlf=true normalizes on commit — that is a property of one
#      contributor's git config, not of the repo. Now it is the repo's.

*                     text=auto

# Generated, hashed, published — LF always.
*.html                text eol=lf
*.json                text eol=lf
corpus/mirrors/lumberjacks/*.jsonl text eol=lf

# Linux-destined infrastructure — LF always.
*.sh                  text eol=lf
*.sh.tftpl            text eol=lf
*.service             text eol=lf
Caddyfile             text eol=lf
*.yml                 text eol=lf
*.yaml                text eol=lf

# Hashed release artifacts and binaries — never transform.
*.zip                 binary
*.dll                 binary
*.png                 binary
*.jpg                 binary
